# Sales

This is the front matter of a real app — the **manifest** for the [[Welcome to Sales]]
workbook. Every workbook has exactly one of these. It is the file the weave reads
first to learn three things: *what this workbook is*, *what it depends on*, and
*how to build and ship it*. Read it like a table of contents with teeth — every
name below is a `[[backlink]]` that resolves to a real file in this tree, and every
name is also a node the [[The Code Graph]] resolves when it audits the build.

The non-negotiable rule shows itself immediately: **a manifest is not JSON.** There
is no `package.json`, no `sales.toml`, no sidecar config to keep in sync. The
manifest is the same literate text as every other file — declarations and prose.
The elements *are* the source of truth. If you ever reach for a `.json` to describe
a workbook, you are doing it wrong.

## What this workbook is — the tagging edge

Every workbook declares *what it is* at the top. The format's vocabulary for "what
something is" is the **tagging edge** — a small set of facets that can co-occur,
with **kit as the floor**. This workbook is an **app** (it has an entry interface,
the [[Sales Dashboard]]), it bundles **agents** (it ships a [[The Analyst Agent]]
brain and friends), and it **exports a kit** ([[FinModel — a kit the whole team imports]])
that other workbooks can import. `container` is *not* in this list — that is an
execution property of a unit, not a type of the workbook.

The declaration is a plain struct value. Field types are inferred from the defaults,
so `name` is a string, `facets` is a list of atoms, and `exports` is a list of
module references. No DSL, no schema.

defmodule Workbook do
  defstruct name: "",
            tagline: "",
            facets: [],
            entry: nil,
            exports: [],
            version: "0.0.0"
end

workbook = %Workbook{
  name: "Sales",
  tagline: "A lead-intelligence workbook: ingest, enrich, score, rank, draft, ship.",
  facets: [:app, :agent, :kit],
  entry: "02-pages/dashboard.work",
  exports: [FinModel],
  version: "0.4.0"
}

The `:entry` is the leaf the desktop app launches and the deploy lane serves as the
default region — the [[Sales Dashboard]]. The `:exports` list is the workbook's
*public* surface: only `FinModel` crosses the boundary, so a sibling workbook that
`imports Sales` sees `ltv/1`, `cac/1`, and `npv/2`, and nothing else. Everything
else in the tree is internal.

## Dependencies and packages

A workbook composes other workbooks and a handful of client engines. Dependencies
are addressed *by name*, the way every reference in this format is — not by a URL
buried in a lockfile. Each entry is a tagged tuple: the name, the version
constraint, and where the weave resolves it from.

deps = [
  {:finmodel,   "~> 0.4", path: "kits/finmodel.work"},
  {:work_ui,    "~> 1.0", kit: "work-*"},
  {:svelte,     "~> 5.0", island: :client},
  {:solid,      "~> 1.8", island: :client}
]

The first line is the interesting one: a workbook can depend on **itself** —
`:finmodel` is the [[FinModel — a kit the whole team imports]] kit this very tree
exports, resolved by `path:`. That is the dogfood loop made literal. `:work_ui` is
the pre-built `work-*` component library every page composes. `:svelte` and `:solid`
are the two client island runtimes the weave hydrates in the browser — they back
the [[Score Chart]] and [[Sparkline]] units respectively.

Foreign-language compute units do not appear here as packages. The [[Forecast]]
unit's Rust and Zig, and the [[Risk]] unit's Python, are resolved by the compiler
lane at weave time — the source is *inline* in those files, compiled to wasm in
their sandboxes. There is nothing to add to a dependency list; the unit *is* the
dependency.

## The nexus this workbook targets

The optional server tier is the **nexus** — the trusted backend that runs native
Elixir on the BEAM, backs the agents, holds the data, and brokers the [[The Contract]]
capability seam. The manifest names which nexus this workbook talks to and how. The
same authored workbook can target a local nexus in development and a cloud nexus in
production; nothing in the units changes, only this routing config.

nexus = %{
  dev:  [provider: :local,   endpoint: "rcp://localhost:4000",      tier: :native_beam],
  prod: [provider: :runtime, endpoint: "rcp://sales.nexus.work",    tier: :native_beam]
}

The `:provider` atom is the only thing that differs, and it is exactly the
distinction [[The Contract]] is built around: `:local` fulfils a capability from the
OS or browser-native APIs, `:runtime` fulfils it from the shared server over RCP.
A *target* — desktop, web, mobile — is just a choice of provider plus an endpoint.
It is **never** a code fork, and the nexus is **never** wasm: native BEAM on the
server, with foreign units sandboxed beside it.

## Publish posture — the three delivery modes

The same workbook ships three ways with no rewrite, and the manifest records which
one this build is publishing under. This is the locked delivery model, named:

publish = %{
  mode:    :static,
  modes: [
    static: [out: "salesapp.html", hydrate: :client],
    ssr:    [render: :nexus, islands: :client],
    live:   [render: :nexus, diffs: "rcp/ws"]
  ],
  visibility: :private,
  registry:   "sales.nexus.work"
}

The default `:static` mode is what [[Deploy]] emits: the whole tree woven into one
self-contained gzipped `salesapp.html` that hydrates entirely in the browser, no
nexus required to view it. Flip `mode:` to `:ssr` and the server regions render on
the BEAM while client units stay islands; flip to `:live` and the nexus streams
diffs over the RCP websocket. One authoring model, every delivery mode — the choice
lives *here*, in one line, not in three different builds.

## The weave checks — a red test ships no bundle

The last job of the manifest is to declare the gate. `work bundle` does not just
concatenate files: it runs the **weave gate**, and the gate is allowed to fail the
build. These are the checks it must pass, declared as a list of atoms — the enum of
gates this workbook insists on.

@gates [
  :resolve_refs,     # every [[backlink]], :atom, and @type points at a real node
  :audit_caps,       # no unit uses a capability its sandbox grant did not give it
  :audit_deps,       # the dependency graph is acyclic and fully resolved
  :run_tests,        # every unit's inline tests pass — red test, no bundle
  :validate_tasks    # every Task owner resolves to a defined person
]

weave = %{
  gates:   @gates,
  graph:   "90-graph/graph.work",
  on_fail: :block
}

`:on_fail` is `:block` — the [[The Weave]] unit explains why this is the whole point.
A workbook whose [[Plan]] references an undefined owner, or whose [[Risk]] unit
reaches for a capability its sandbox never granted, must not produce a shippable
artifact. The gate runs `work check` over the [[The Code Graph]], and the graph is
where `:resolve_refs` and `:audit_caps` get their answers.

## The tree

The folders below are the workbook, in reading order. Each is a real stage of the
app, and each teaches the format concepts it needs while it does its job.

- **01-foundation** — the front door. [[Welcome to Sales]] explains the four lanes;
  [[Lead]] declares the canonical `%Lead{}` struct and the `@statuses` enum; the
  glossary fixes the vocabulary.
- **02-pages** — the leaf interface. [[Sales Dashboard]] is the `:entry` this
  manifest names: the page a user launches, composed from `work-*` elements.
- **03-lib** — the server compute. [[Scoring a lead]] (`:score`), [[Enrich]]
  (`:enrich`, granted net to `api.crm.com`), and the [[Pipeline]] flow that wires
  seed → enrich → score → rank.
- **04-sandboxing** — isolation as a named unit. [[Pricing under a sandbox]] puts
  `:tax` inside `sandbox :pricing`, the canonical example of a capability boundary.
- **05-data** — typed content. [[Leads — the typed data source]] declares the seed
  leads as `data` of `Lead`, queryable and rendered by default.
- **06-dock** — the capability seam. [[The Contract]] is the one file that shows the
  *generated* WIT — clearly labelled as the artifact you never touch.
- **07-agents** — the runtime-backed brains. [[The Analyst Agent]] drafts outreach,
  the coder runs ReAct, the researcher loops plan → search → verify, and
  [[Reflexion: an outreach email that critiques and revises itself]] keeps its trace
  as memory.
- **08-plan** — live state. [[Plan]] declares `Task` values whose `:owner` and
  `:status` are validated at the weave gate.
- **09-weave** — the polyglot edge and the build itself: the [[Score Chart]] Svelte
  island, the [[Sparkline]] Solid island, the [[Forecast]] Rust+Zig sandbox, the
  [[Risk]] Python sandbox, all pulled together by [[The Weave]].
- **10-deploy** — shipping. [[Deploy]] emits `salesapp.html` and explains the three
  delivery modes this manifest's `publish` block selects between.
- **90-graph** — the spine. [[The Code Graph]] is the typed property graph every
  gate above queries.
- **kits** — what we export. [[FinModel — a kit the whole team imports]] is the one
  module in `:exports`, the reusable financial model the whole team imports.

To build the whole thing from this manifest, the weave does what the rest of this
file declared: resolve every reference in the tree, audit the caps and deps, run the
tests, and — only if the gate stays green — emit the one file.

flow :build do
  step :load,    do: Weave.read(workbook)
  step :resolve, do: Graph.resolve(weave.graph)
  step :audit,   do: Weave.audit(@gates)
  step :test,    do: Weave.run_tests()
  step :emit,    do: Weave.bundle(publish, out: "salesapp.html")
end
